course: dictionary
lesson: 7244
title: Pickpockets in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Context helps you work out new words. «Pickpockets look for open bags and back pockets in busy places, because money there is easy to take.» The clues are open bags, back pockets and easy to take, so a pickpocket must be someone who steals from people without them noticing.
explain: Pickpockets work in crowds, on trains, in markets and at festivals, where it is easy to get lost among people. That is why we are told to keep wallets and phones in front pockets or zipped bags.
explain: Pickpocket is a noun for a person. It can also be a rare verb, meaning to steal from someone's pocket in this way. Pickpocketing is a crime, so it is always wrong.
fact: A pickpocket is a thief who steals money, phones or wallets without the owner noticing.
fact: Pickpockets often work in crowds, where it is hard to notice who is standing close to you.
fact: In busy places, keep wallets and phones in front pockets or zipped bags.
fact: Pickpocketing is a crime.
fact: In stories such as Oliver Twist, pickpockets are often clever and quick-fingered thieves.
exercise: «Pickpockets look for open bags and back pockets in busy places.» Who are pickpockets? | Shop workers who count the money | Police officers who guard busy places | *Thieves who steal from pockets and bags without people noticing
exercise: Why do pickpockets work in busy places? | Because they like loud noises | *Because it is easy to take things in a crowd without being noticed | Because shops and markets are closed then
exercise: Safety tip: where should you keep your wallet in a crowd? | Loose in your hand while you run | *In a front pocket or a zipped bag | On a table in a café
test: «Beware of pickpockets» tells you to do what? | Ask them to help you carry your bags | *Be careful and keep your things safe | Give them your money to keep | Look for a new pocket
test: A pickpocket took 3 coins from a bag of 10 coins. How many coins are left? | *7 | 3 | 10 | 13
exam: Which words best describe a pickpocket? | *A thief who steals from pockets and bags in crowds | A bright shining lamp | A kind helper who looks after bags | A busy market with many stalls
